---
import InfoLayout from '../layouts/InfoLayout.astro';
import FirstViewport from '../components/site-standard/FirstViewport.astro';
import Section from '../components/site-standard/Section.astro';
import StatusBadge from '../components/site-standard/StatusBadge.astro';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../utils/seo';

const pageTitle = `Content Update Policy — ${SITE_NAME}`;
const pageDesc = 'How CryptoBonusWorld handles evidence freshness, re-verification, stale exchange information, corrections and country-specific review states.';
---

<InfoLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDesc} canonical="/update-policy/">
  <FirstViewport
    family="trust"
    container="standard"
    eyebrow="Update policy"
    title="Freshness is part of every material claim."
    lede="Exchange, country and offer information can change at different speeds. CryptoBonusWorld keeps review timing attached to the evidence instead of treating one page date as proof that every claim is current."
  >
    <div class="update-meta">
      <StatusBadge label="Checked dates" tone="verified" />
      <StatusBadge label="Stale fails closed" tone="review" />
      <StatusBadge label="Conflict blocks promotion" tone="blocked" />
      <span>Reviewed 10 Aug 2026</span>
    </div>
  </FirstViewport>

  <Section container="prose" spacing="standard" tone="primary">
    <div class="cbw-prose">
      <h2>What “current” means</h2>
      <p>
        A page can contain several kinds of information with different lifetimes: exchange identity, licensing, product access, payment rails,
        country restrictions, promo codes and campaign conditions. A recent page build does not make all of those facts fresh.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a material claim depends on evidence, its record carries its own checked or review timing. Public presentation is allowed to become more cautious when that timing expires.
      </p>

      <h2>What receives tighter review</h2>
      <div class="freshness-grid cbw-card-grid cbw-card-grid--2">
        <article class="cbw-card"><strong>Commercial offers</strong><p>Promo codes, campaign terms, landing pages and referral routes can change quickly and require frequent rechecks.</p></article>
        <article class="cbw-card"><strong>Country availability</strong><p>Licensing, restrictions, local onboarding and product access are reviewed as country-bound evidence, not inferred from global pages.</p></article>
        <article class="cbw-card"><strong>Payments and products</strong><p>Fiat rails, P2P methods, withdrawals and product availability can vary by account or jurisdiction and keep their limitations.</p></article>
        <article class="cbw-card"><strong>Stable identity</strong><p>Basic exchange identity changes less often, but material entity or legal changes still trigger a new review.</p></article>
      </div>

      <h2>When a record becomes stale</h2>
      <p>
        Stale data is not automatically carried forward as if it were verified. Depending on the claim, the public state may become under review,
        lose an action, fall back to a global context, or be withheld until evidence is refreshed.
      </p>

      <h2>Event-triggered review</h2>
      <p>Review can be triggered before a normal review window when there is evidence of a material change, for example:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>an exchange changes a promotion, legal entity, product or country restriction;</li>
        <li>a regulator or government source changes a relevant status;</li>
        <li>a commercial destination stops resolving to the expected exchange-owned page;</li>
        <li>two authoritative sources materially conflict;</li>
        <li>a user or reviewer reports a concrete discrepancy that can be checked.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What “Last verified” should mean</h2>
      <p>
        A displayed verification date should come from the evidence record that supports the visible claim. It should not be manufactured from a page build date,
        a human editorial label, or a different exchange's evidence. If exact supporting evidence is unavailable, the interface should say that the item is being reviewed instead of inventing a checked date.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Confirmed errors are corrected at the source record or content layer that owns the fact, then dependent public surfaces are rebuilt from that corrected state.
        Material corrections should not be hidden by leaving an older verified label or action in place.
      </p>

      <h2>Report a problem</h2>
      <p>
        If you find a stale claim, a broken official reference or a mismatch between the site and an exchange-owned source, use the <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a>.
        For the underlying evidence rules, see <a href="/methodology/">How We Verify</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
</InfoLayout>

<style>
  .update-meta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--cbw-space-2); align-items: center; margin-top: var(--cbw-space-4); color: rgba(255,255,255,.58); font-size: 10px; }
  .freshness-grid { margin-block: var(--cbw-space-5); }
  .freshness-grid article { margin: 0; }
  .freshness-grid strong { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--cbw-space-2); color: var(--cbw-text-primary); font-size: 13px; }
  .freshness-grid p { margin: 0; font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.6; }
</style>
